>
  <p>Traverse is one product runtime and one host ABI, shared across hosts that have actually shipped. People call that contract <code>runtime.wasm</code>. WASM is how the sandbox and portable artifacts work. It is not the product identity, and it is not a license to claim every OS and cloud placement.</p>

  <h2>Embedders are clients</h2>
  <p>The browser package, native Rust embedder, CLI, and MCP server talk to one runtime contract. They do not each reinvent a separate Traverse runtime in their language. When you call a capability from npm, crates.io, or an MCP client, you are asking that runtime to discover → execute → trace against a known contract.</p>
  <p>Shared means the contract and the host ABI. It does not mean every package ships the same <code>runtime.wasm</code> bytes. <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code> loads the bundle’s digest-checked <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code>. <code>traverse-embedder@0.15.0</code> links <code>traverse-runtime</code> natively and ships no separate runtime-WASM file. The walk-through is <a href="/blog/one-shared-runtime-two-loading-paths.html">one shared runtime, two loading paths</a>.</p>

  <h2>Honest consumers today</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>JS/TS</strong> — published npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code></li>
    <li><strong>Rust</strong> — published crates.io <code>traverse-embedder@0.15.0</code></li>
    <li><strong>AI agents</strong> — published <code>traverse-mcp</code> (stdio bootstrap)</li>
    <li><strong>Python</strong> — works by shelling out to <code>traverse-cli capability-package execute</code>. There is <strong>no Python SDK</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Swift / Kotlin / .NET</strong> — published, pre-1.0, marked In progress, not Certified. Swift xcframework on <code>swift-host-v*</code> tags; Kotlin <code>com.traverse-framework:traverse-embedder</code> 0.15.0 on Maven Central; .NET <code>TraverseEmbedder</code> 0.15.0 on nuget.org.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Edge is planned. Cloud placement is an explicit non-goal for v0.1. Before assuming a host works, read <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> and <a href="/what-is-real-today.html">what is real today</a>.</p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>It is not “write once, run on any OS forever.” It is one governed capability on the hosts Traverse has actually shipped. Capability WASM and the shared runtime are different layers: your business rule is a capability artifact; the orchestrator that validates and runs it is that one runtime contract.</p>
